    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 262307 RRA\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n407 PM PDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dangerous heat expected across the Mojave Desert through Friday. \n\n* Chances of showers and thunderstorms across the region today \n  through Saturday, with gusty winds and heavy rain being the \n  greatest threat.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Early Next Week\n\nWell above normal temperatures continue through the rest of the week \nacross the Mojave Desert as strong 500 mb ridging is centered over \nthe Four Corners region. Upper air observations indicate 597dm \nheights under the ridge, which is well above average for this time \nof year. In addition well above normal low and mid level temperature \nanomalies support extreme heat persisting through at least the \nweekend. Wednesday and Thursday are expected to be the hottest days \nof the week with most areas seeing readings about 8-10 degrees above \nnormal. An approaching trough over the eastern Pacific will help to \nsuppress the ridge and shift it eastward Friday through the weekend, \nresulting in lowering heights and slightly cooler temperatures. \nDespite a slow downward trend in temperatures into the weekend, they \nwill still remain several degrees above normal for late summer. An \nExtreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Friday for the Mojave \nDesert. While afternoon high temperatures have been this hot (or \nhotter) earlier in the summer without heat headlines, it is \nimportant to note that there is historically a resurgence of heat-\nrelated impacts at the end of the season. See the CLIMATE section at \nthe bottom for daily records. \n\nDespite the presence of extreme heat, monsoon season is still here \nwith chances for showers and storms today through Saturday for \nportions of NW Arizona, the Colorado River Valley, and Southern \nNevada. Deepest moisture today remains over the lower Colorado River \nValley and within Mohave County where PW remains near 1.00\" but \nsurface dew points are only in the 40s to near 50. With marginal \ninstability and moisture today, expect any convection to be mostly \nover higher terrain. Deeper moisture surges northward Thursday \nthrough Saturday with dew points in the 50s and 60s and PW in the \n1.00 to 1.40 inch range. An approaching trough will also bring \nstronger upper level winds from 700mb up to 300mb, resulting in \nbetter shear profiles for convective organization. While the overall \ndaily storm coverage can easily be impacted by leftover clouds from \nthe previous days convection, confidence is increasing in at least \nscattered storm chances each afternoon Thursday - Saturday for much \nof the region. Higher terrain will have the best chances for storms \nwith outflow and leftover boundaries working to initiate convective \ndevelopment elsewhere. Any storm will have the capability of \nproducing heavy rain, flooding, strong winds, and lightning. Mid \nrange guidance does show drier air advecting into the region Sunday \ninto early next week likely keeping monsoon activity at bay for a \nfew days. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast \nPackage...Typical light wind patterns are expected through the \nperiod as east winds under 8 KT this afternoon become southwest \ntonight. Light and variable winds Thursday morning will become \nsoutheast late morning then southwest in the afternoon. A few gusts \nto around 15KT are possible with the southeast winds before more \nfrequent gusts 15-20KT set up with the southwest winds in the \nafternoon. Isolated showers and thunderstorms ongoing this afternoon \nwill diminish quickly this evening, mainly remaining south and north \nof the terminals. After a dry night, more widespread convection is \nexpected to develop Thursday afternoon, including near the \nterminals. Gusty erratic winds are the main concern with storms, \nwith lightning, brief heavy rain, and CIGs below 10kft also \npossible. Showers and thunderstorms Thursday will diminish after \nsunset. Temperatures will exceed 100F through 04Z this evening then \nagain 17Z-04Z tomorrow. VFR skies prevailing. \n  \nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Typical wind patterns \nare expected tonight with winds generally under 10KT. KDAG will \nbriefly gust out of the west to around 20KT this evening like they \nusually do, but gusts should end by 09Z tonight. Isolated showers \nand thunderstorms will continue in the terrain of Southern Nevada \nand western Arizona this afternoon, diminishing after sunset. On \nThursday, south to southeast winds will increase in  the early \nafternoon across much of the region with gusts 15-25KT expected. \nConvection will develop in Southern Nevada and Western Arizona with \nmore widespread coverage and higher risk for impacts than the past \nfew days. Gusty erratic winds, lightning, and CIGs below 10kft are \npossible with any storms. Further northwest in Inyo County, mainly \nshowers are expected. VFR skies prevailing through the period.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     109(2001)*  110(2017)*  110(2019)*\nBishop        103(2019)*  103(2019)*  103(2017)\nNeedles       122(1924)   120(1891)   116(2023)*\nDaggett       111(2011)   111(2017)   111(2017)\nKingman       106(2020)*  107(2017)*  107(2009)\nDesert Rock   106(2001)*  107(2017)   107(2017)\nDeath Valley  122(2017)*  124(1924)   120(2011)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     88(2020)*   87(2011)*   86(2019)* \nBishop        66(1988)    67(1970)    62(1970) \nNeedles       93(2011)    93(2011)    94(2017) \nDaggett       82(1981)*   81(2011)*   82(2017)* \nKingman       79(1945)    79(2020)*   75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   78(1985)*   78(1995)*   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley  98(1991)*   95(2011)*  100(2019)* \n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Nickerson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
